⸻

1. PAGE STRUCTURE: WHAT GOES WHERE (VERY SPECIFIC)

The page is built in layers, not sections.

Think in Z-axis, not Y-axis.

Layer stack (bottom → top)
	1.	Dark base
	•	Solid black / near-black background
	•	No texture at first
	2.	Primary image layer
	•	One large, abstract, cinematic image
	•	Never centered perfectly
	•	Slightly off-axis (top-left, top-right, or bleeding off screen)
	3.	Gradient veil
	•	Dark gradient covering parts of the image
	•	Ensures image never dominates
	4.	Glass / blur layer
	•	Subtle glass card or blur patch
	•	Only where text exists
	5.	Text / minimal UI
	•	Sparse
	•	Calm
	•	Never boxed aggressively

This is why the reference app looks “expensive”.

⸻

2. IMAGE PLACEMENT RULES (THIS IS THE CORE)

Image size
	•	Images are large
	•	Often bigger than the viewport
	•	Cropped intentionally

This creates presence.

Image position
	•	Never perfectly centered
	•	Slightly drifting:
	•	top-left with overflow
	•	top-right with fade
	•	diagonal tension

Example placements:
	•	Planet half-visible on the right
	•	Abstract art bleeding from top
	•	Subject partially obscured by darkness

Image interaction with UI
	•	UI NEVER sits directly on raw image
	•	Always separated by:
	•	dark gradient
	•	glass blur
	•	opacity reduction

The image is behind reality, not part of it.

⸻

3. WHAT KIND OF IMAGES TO USE (CRITICAL)

Tell your designer this exactly:

Image qualities
	•	Cinematic
	•	Dream-like
	•	Slightly surreal
	•	Low contrast
	•	Muted saturation
	•	Soft focus or motion blur
	•	Film grain (very subtle)

Avoid:
	•	Stock photos
	•	Sharp faces
	•	Bright whites
	•	High-detail realism

Think:

memory, reflection, future, distance

⸻

4. GRADIENT SYSTEM (VERY CONTROLLED)

Gradients are not decoration.
They simulate light and depth.

Use ONLY these gradients:

A. Darkness gradient (most common)
	•	Black → transparent
	•	Vertical or diagonal
	•	Covers images partially
	•	Purpose: readability + mystery

B. Ambient glow gradient (rare)
	•	Radial
	•	Dark orange
	•	Very low opacity (5–10%)
	•	Large blur
	•	Never sharp

Used to suggest:
	•	importance
	•	energy
	•	presence

🚫 No rainbow gradients
🚫 No colorful hero gradients
🚫 No gradients inside buttons

Gradients should feel like light leakage, not branding.

⸻

5. COLOR PHILOSOPHY (WHY IT WORKS)

Dominant colors
	•	Black
	•	Near-black
	•	Soft white

Accent color (dark orange)
	•	Used once per screen max
	•	Never full saturation
	•	Often blurred or glowing
	•	Rare = powerful

Orange means:
	•	action
	•	pulse
	•	future
	•	decision

If orange becomes common, the entire system collapses.

⸻

6. HOW TEXT SHOULD LIVE ON THE PAGE

Text placement
	•	Not centered vertically
	•	Usually lower third or upper third
	•	Feels editorial, like a magazine cover

Text behavior
	•	Short statements
	•	Calm tone
	•	Space around text is intentional

Example:
	•	One headline
	•	One supporting line
	•	Nothing else

No paragraphs. No clutter.

⸻

7. WHAT “NO UI YET” ACTUALLY MEANS (IMPORTANT)

“No UI” doesn’t mean empty.

It means:
	•	No cards
	•	No feature grids
	•	No nav shouting for attention
	•	No icons everywhere

Instead:
	•	One idea per screen
	•	One image
	•	One emotional message

The reference app delays UI to build trust.

⸻

8. WHY THIS STYLE FEELS PREMIUM

Because it communicates:
	•	restraint
	•	confidence
	•	patience

Cheap products:
	•	explain everything
	•	show everything
	•	ask for attention

Premium products:
	•	reveal slowly
	•	hide complexity
	•	let silence speak

